# Publish-gate suppressions for rite's own repo — `rite publish check`.
#
# Format is `<fingerprint>  # <reason>`, one per line, and the reason is
# mandatory (gate.suppression.parse rejects a line without one). There is no
# global off switch by design (SPEC §11.4); every line below is a decision
# about one specific finding.
#
# Every entry BELOW is pinned to WHAT was found —
# `commit:file:rule:sha256-<digest>` — rather than to where it sits. The
# line-numbered form gitleaks reports is still accepted and an existing file
# full of it needs no migration; it identifies a finding by a proxy that
# drifts, which is why this file no longer uses it:
# insert a line anywhere above a suppressed one and the entry matches nothing,
# so the finding it covered blocks the push and the entry is reported stale,
# with nothing about the suppressed code changed. That is not hypothetical:
# three added comment lines in gate.py moved a suppressed docstring by three
# lines and failed the gate on a decision already made — measured twice, once
# by accident mid-push and once deliberately, to check this fix.
#
# `rite publish check` prints the content form under every finding that has
# one, along with this file's name and the shape of a line. Copy what it
# prints; there is nothing to compute by hand.
#
# Two consequences worth knowing before you write one:
#
#   * The digest is the matched TEXT, so two identical matches of one rule in
#     one file share an entry — one reason covers both. The gate says so
#     ("one entry covers N findings") rather than letting a decision quietly
#     grow to cover an occurrence nobody looked at.
#   * A digest is a commitment to that text, and for the low-entropy shapes
#     these rules catch — a home path, a username — it is a confirmable
#     guess, not a one-way function. That is harmless while the entry is
#     live, because a suppressed string is by definition one this project
#     decided to keep in the open and is sitting in the tracked tree. It
#     stops being harmless if you SCRUB the string and leave the entry: then
#     the file commits to something no longer public. Delete the entry in the
#     same change. The gate reports exactly that entry as stale, which is the
#     prompt to do it.
#
# ── Before publishing: regenerating this file after the squash ─────────
# Content-pinning fixes the LINE half of a fingerprint, not the commit half.
# Two kinds of entry here depend on a commit SHA, and squashing history to a single initial commit
# does DIFFERENT things to them — which is why this is a procedure and not
# one `sed`:
#
#   * entries for a finding in a FILE, pinned to the commit that introduced
#     it. The squash does not remove the content, so the same finding
#     reappears under the new commit's SHA: the gate blocks (exit 2) and the
#     old entry goes stale (exit 1). These must be RE-POINTED — the SHA only;
#     the digest is the same text and does not change.
#   * entries for a finding in a `<commit message>`. A squash replaces the
#     messages, so the finding ceases to exist. These must be DELETED.
#     Re-pointing one suppresses nothing and leaves a stale entry behind.
#
# Commit 8d411344… carries one of EACH, so a `sed` keyed on that SHA alone
# would wrongly re-point the commit-message entry too. Delete first, then
# re-point what is left:
#
#     # 1. drop every commit-message entry — the squash removes the findings
#     # 2. re-point every remaining commit-pinned entry at the new commit
#     NEW=$(git rev-parse HEAD)
#     sed -i '' -E "s/^[0-9a-f]{40}:/$NEW:/" .rite/gitleaksignore
#     # 3. the gate is the check, not the diff
#     rite publish check          # must exit 0
#
# Written to match by SHAPE, not by listing today's SHAs, so it keeps working
# as entries are added — the enumeration it replaced already said "these
# three lines" when there were five.
#
# ⚠ "Squash" here means the old commits are actually GONE. gitleaks scans
# every reachable commit, not the current branch — measured twice while
# rehearsing this: `git checkout --orphan` plus a commit leaves the old
# history reachable through the other refs, the gate goes on reporting the
# ORIGINAL SHAs, and step 2 has just re-pointed the entries away from them.
# The result is blocking findings AND stale entries at the same time. Verify
# with `git rev-list --all --count` — it must print 1 — BEFORE step 2.
#
# ⚠ Step 3 can still fail for a reason none of this fixes: if the squashed
# commit's own MESSAGE quotes a home-shaped path, that is a new finding in a
# new message. Write the message first, then run the gate, then suppress
# what it reports.
#
# The `-:` entries are working-tree scans, not history scans, and survive the
# squash untouched. Moving one within its file no longer invalidates it; only
# changing the matched text itself does.

